# CHAPTER 1 — all seven sections, subheadings and extension boxes.
L(1,'1.1.1 Indian Economy prior to British Rule','India before British rule',
'India had a prosperous handicraft sector and a largely self-contained rural economy. Cultivators produced crops, while village artisans and service providers met many local needs. Agriculture relied on traditional methods, but the textbook distinguishes this economy from the later colonial stagnation.',
'**Handicrafts and foreign trade:** fine cotton and silk textiles, metal work and precious-stone products enjoyed an international reputation. Finished handicrafts were exported and bullion—gold and silver—flowed into India. Bengal’s muslin, including fine malmal associated with royal use, illustrates the quality of specialised Indian production.',
'The important comparison is the change in economic structure: before colonial transformation, India exported valued finished products; under British rule, it increasingly supplied raw materials and imported manufactures.')
L(1,'1.1.2 Exploitation and Transformation during British Rule','The two sides of colonial exploitation',
'Colonial exploitation aimed to maximise Britain’s economic advantage. **Supply aspect:** India supplied cheap raw materials such as cotton and jute for British factories. **Demand aspect:** India became a market for British manufactured goods. Destroying competing Indian handicrafts helped achieve both objectives.',
'Agriculture was subjected to heavy land-revenue demands. Cultivators could lose rights when unable to meet them, while intermediaries extracted surplus without investing adequately in farming. The transformation was therefore not simply a change in trade: it weakened the productive structure and the cultivator’s ability to improve it.',
'For a question on the colonial objective, state both sides—raw-material supply and a market for British goods—then explain how policies in agriculture, industry and trade served that objective.')
L(1,'1.1.3 Features on the Eve of Independence','Seven overall features of the colonial economy',
'**Stagnant economy:** real output and per-capita income grew very slowly. **Backward economy:** low productivity, poverty, unemployment and inadequate basic necessities kept living standards low. Stagnation concerns the pace of change; backwardness concerns the economy’s low level of development.',
'**Dominance of agriculture:** about 85% of people depended directly or indirectly on agriculture, while roughly 70–75% of workers were employed in the primary sector. **Bleak industrialisation:** handicrafts declined and modern heavy/capital-goods industries remained inadequate.',
'**Heavy dependence on imports:** machinery, defence requirements and many consumer goods had to be imported. **Limited urbanisation:** most people lived in villages with few opportunities outside farming. **Semi-feudal economy:** landlords controlled land and appropriated surplus, while cultivators retained little beyond subsistence. These features reinforced one another.')
L(1,'1.1 Overall Features · Ability Zone','National-income estimates and the meaning of stagnation',
'No comprehensive, regular official national-income series was developed under colonial rule. Individual estimates were attempted by Dadabhai Naoroji, William Digby, Findlay Shirras, V.K.R.V. Rao and R.C. Desai. Their estimates differed, but Rao’s work is described as especially systematic.',
'The chapter’s Ability Zone cites approximately 2% national-income growth and 0.5% per-capita-income growth during the first half of the twentieth century. Treat these as historical estimates rather than a precise modern series. Per-capita income grows slowly when output barely outpaces population.',
'This explains why a modest increase in total output need not produce a meaningful improvement in the average person’s living conditions.',kind='Box concept')
L(1,'1.2 Agricultural Sector on the Eve of Independence','The seven features of colonial agriculture',
'**Low production and productivity:** total output was small and output per hectare was low. **Exploitative land settlement:** revenue arrangements favoured intermediaries rather than cultivators. **Forced commercialisation:** farmers were pushed towards crops needed by British industry.',
'**Lack of investment in agricultural infrastructure:** irrigation, improved technology, drainage, terracing and flood protection were neglected. Dependence on rainfall made farming uncertain. **Gulf between owners and tillers:** owners received surplus without sharing cultivation costs, while actual cultivators lacked funds to invest.',
'**Small and fragmented holdings:** scattered, uneconomic plots raised cultivation costs and made improved methods difficult. **Subsistence outlook:** farming was mainly a way to feed the family, rather than a profit-oriented enterprise. Together these explain why agriculture could employ most workers and still produce widespread poverty.')
L(1,'1.2 Exploitative Land Settlement System','How the zamindari system kept agriculture stagnant',
'Under the Permanent Settlement in the Bengal Presidency, zamindars were recognised as landowners and paid a fixed revenue amount to the government. They could extract rent from cultivators, and their main interest was collection rather than improvement of land.',
'**Cultivators lacked means:** rent left them with little surplus, pushing them towards debt and subsistence. **Owners lacked incentive:** landlords could receive income without improving cultivation and often spent it on conspicuous consumption. The people doing the work and the people receiving the surplus were different.',
'This is the investment trap: cultivators wanted better output but could not finance improvements; owners had resources but little reason to invest. Insecure rights, exploitation and low investment sustained low productivity.')
L(1,'1.2 Forced Commercialisation · Highlights','Commercial crops, famines and Partition',
'**Commercialisation** means growing for sale rather than self-consumption. Under colonial rule, farmers were forced towards crops such as indigo for British textiles. Market production exposed subsistence farmers to price fluctuations and could reduce the land available for food crops; higher commercial output did not guarantee higher farmer welfare.',
'**Famines:** drought, poor purchasing power, inadequate public-health services and weak relief made crop failures devastating. The highlights discuss repeated famines, including Bengal in 1769–70 and the famine of 1943. Rail transport later made moving relief food easier, but it did not eliminate colonial famine suffering.',
'**Partition:** India received about 82% of the population but only about 65% of the foodgrain area in the chapter’s figures. Productive areas of West Punjab and Sindh went to Pakistan, aggravating food shortages. This is a separate shock from the earlier colonial policies.',kind='Box concept')
L(1,'1.3.1 Systematic De-industrialisation','Five reasons Indian handicrafts declined',
'**Discriminatory tariff policy:** British manufactured goods entered Indian markets on favourable terms, Indian handicraft exports faced heavy duties, and raw-material exports to Britain were encouraged. This weakened Indian goods both at home and abroad.',
'**Expansion of railways:** cheap British products reached the interior. **Low cost of British goods:** machinery and large-scale production made imports difficult for handmade goods to compete with. **Disappearance of Indian kingdoms:** the decline of royal courts removed important patrons of specialised crafts.',
'**Loss of skilled artisans and lack of capital:** declining demand and low returns forced artisans out of their trades and reduced investment. De-industrialisation means this decline was not offset by enough modern industrial employment. Consequences included unemployment, loss of skills, pressure on agriculture and a larger market for British manufactures.')
L(1,'1.3.2 Growth of Modern Industry during 1850–1947','Modern industry: five observations',
'**Textile mills:** cotton mills developed mainly in western India and were largely Indian-owned; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal and largely foreign-owned. **Iron and steel:** TISCO was established in 1907; other branches developed gradually, including sugar, cement and paper.',
'**Weak capital-goods industry:** machinery and industrial plants were not produced on the scale needed for broad industrialisation. **Small overall contribution:** modern industry’s contribution to output and employment was too limited to replace the lost handicraft base.',
'**Limited state participation:** government activity was concentrated in railways, power, communications, ports and selected departmental undertakings. The conclusion is limited and uneven modernisation—not the complete absence of factories.')
L(1,'1.4 Foreign Trade on the Eve of Independence','Three characteristics of colonial foreign trade',
'**Exporter of raw materials, importer of finished goods:** exports included raw cotton, silk, wool, jute, indigo and sugar; imports included manufactured consumer goods and machinery. This reversed the earlier reputation for exporting finished handicrafts.',
'**Monopoly control:** more than half of foreign trade was directed towards Britain; other partners included China, Ceylon and Persia. Britain largely determined the composition and direction of trade.',
'**Surplus trade benefiting Britain:** an export surplus did not bring equivalent domestic prosperity. It financed administrative expenses, British war expenditure and payments on behalf of the colonial government. Essential supplies could remain scarce in India while exports generated resources used abroad. This is the drain of wealth.')
L(1,'1.4 Highlights · Suez Canal','Suez Canal and access to Indian markets',
'The Suez Canal opened in 1869 and provided a shorter sea route between Europe and Asia, avoiding the longer journey around Africa. Lower transport costs made trading between Britain and India easier.',
'In the colonial setting, this strengthened Britain’s control over Indian trade and made British goods more competitive in Indian markets. The general benefit of a cheaper transport route must therefore be distinguished from who controlled the gains.',kind='Box concept')
L(1,'1.5 Demographic Profile','Birth, death, infant mortality, life expectancy and literacy',
'**Birth and death rates:** approximately 48 and 40 per thousand respectively in the chapter’s historical account. Both were high, so the gap—and therefore population growth—was initially small. Poverty, poor sanitation, recurrent disease and inadequate healthcare kept mortality high.',
'**Infant mortality:** about 218 deaths below age one per 1,000 live births. **Life expectancy:** roughly 32 years. These indicate poor public health and living conditions. Infant mortality uses live births as its denominator, not the total population.',
'**Literacy:** below 16%, with female literacy around 7%. The very low female rate also reflects gender bias. Explain what the indicators reveal rather than presenting numbers alone; all figures here describe the historical period, not present-day India.')
L(1,'1.5 Highlights · Census and Demographic Transition','The census, 1921 and maintenance investment',
'The chapter identifies 1881 as the beginning of regular decennial population censuses. A census records population size and its demographic characteristics, enabling comparison over time.',
'**1921—the Year of the Great Divide:** before it, population growth fluctuated, with increases and decreases across censuses. Afterwards, population rose more consistently. Both birth and death rates remained high until around 1951, keeping growth relatively modest; a subsequent decline in mortality with high births widened the gap.',
'**Maintenance investment** is expenditure needed to maintain the existing population. When population needs absorb a large share of resources, less is available to expand productive capacity. The point is the resource constraint, not that spending on people has no value.',kind='Box concept')
L(1,'1.6 Occupational Structure','Occupational structure and regional differences',
'Occupational structure is the distribution of workers across primary, secondary and tertiary activities. The chapter uses 1951 figures because reliable 1947 figures are unavailable: agriculture employed about 70–75%, industry about 10% and services about 15–20%.',
'**Agriculture as the principal occupation:** heavy dependence reduced land available per worker and reinforced subsistence farming. **Industry as an insignificant occupation:** manufacturing could not absorb surplus workers. **Insignificant transition:** the movement from agriculture to industry was too weak to produce an economic take-off.',
'Regional exceptions existed: parts of the Madras Presidency, Bombay and Bengal showed declining dependence on agriculture; Odisha, Rajasthan and Punjab showed an increase. The overall pattern remained backward and lopsided. Do not confuse workers’ sectoral shares with sectors’ contributions to GDP.')
L(1,'1.7 Infrastructure','Roads, railways, ports, waterways and communications',
'Infrastructure is the support system for growth. Economic infrastructure includes transport, communications, banking and power; social infrastructure includes education, health and housing.',
'**Railways** moved British goods to Indian interiors and raw materials towards ports; the first passenger train ran from Bombay to Thane in 1853. **Ports** handled this trade. **Inland waterways** received limited development; the expensive Odisha coast canal could not compete with railways and was abandoned.',
'**Roads** primarily served military movement and transport of materials. All-weather rural access remained inadequate, worsening hardship during floods and famines. **Post and telegraph** strengthened administration and law and order, while postal services remained insufficient. Some infrastructure developed, but its colonial purpose explains its limited contribution to broad welfare.')
L(1,'1.7.1 Positive Impact · Highlights on Railways','Positive side-effects and a balanced evaluation',
'The textbook lists five positive side-effects: **commercial outlook of farmers**, who became more aware of market prices; **new employment opportunities** in transport and related activity; **better movement of famine relief**; a wider **monetary system of exchange**, supporting specialisation; and an **administrative framework** inherited at independence.',
'Railways also widened domestic markets, encouraged commercialisation, connected distant regions and promoted cultural interaction. Faster movement of food could reduce the impact of shortages. These are benefits of the means used, even though the underlying motive remained exploitation.',
'The negative side was easier export of Indian raw materials and easier distribution of British manufactures. A balanced answer explains both effects and concludes that colonial priorities, rather than Indian development, determined the system.',kind='Box concept')
# CHAPTER 2
L(2,'2.1 Economic Systems','Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies',
'**Capitalist economy:** private ownership, market allocation and profit maximisation. Producers respond to consumers’ purchasing power, so consumer choice and incentives can encourage efficiency and growth. The limitation is that needs without purchasing power may go unmet, and income/wealth can become concentrated.',
'**Socialist economy:** collective/public ownership and allocation by a central authority to maximise social welfare. Production, distribution and prices are directed according to assessed needs. Its strength is the priority given to equity; its limitations include restricted consumer choice and weaker incentives that can reduce efficiency.',
'**Mixed economy:** public and private ownership coexist. Private firms pursue profit within regulation; public activity pursues social welfare. It seeks growth with social justice, but public-sector inefficiency/corruption and weak coordination can undermine results. India chose this approach rather than an extreme version of either system.')
L(2,'2.1 Mixed Economic System · 2.2.1 Planning','Why India adopted planning and a mixed economy',
'India inherited low productivity, poverty, weak industry and scarce capital. Market forces alone were unlikely to undertake the large, coordinated investment needed in infrastructure and basic industries. Government therefore supplied a big push while retaining private property and democracy.',
'Economic planning means using resources in accordance with national priorities: a central authority specifies targets, policies and programmes for a stated period. A plan explains how limited resources will be used to achieve defined objectives. Goals are essential because resources cannot be allocated purposefully without knowing what is to be achieved.',
'The Planning Commission was established in 1950 under Jawaharlal Nehru; the First Five Year Plan began in 1951. A **perspective plan** sets longer-term aims, commonly over 15–25 years, while each Five Year Plan identifies nearer-term priorities. P.C. Mahalanobis, the architect of Indian planning, is especially associated with the Second Plan’s industrial strategy, the Indian Statistical Institute and Sankhya.')
L(2,'2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans','Growth, modernisation, self-reliance, self-sufficiency and equity',
'**Growth:** increase the capacity to produce goods and services. It can come from a larger resource base, more productive capital and supporting services, or better technology. Sustained growth in real GDP indicates an expanding output flow.',
'**Modernisation:** adopt new technology and improve social outlook, including gender equality and women’s participation. **Self-reliance:** reduce dependence on foreign financial resources. **Self-sufficiency:** reduce dependence on foreign supplies of essentials. The goal protected economic decisions from political pressure attached to aid or food imports.',
'**Equity:** spread benefits across society so everyone can meet basic needs—food, clothing, shelter, education and healthcare. A plan may emphasise one goal more than another, but growth without widespread benefits is not enough. The four common headings are growth, modernisation, self-reliance/self-sufficiency and equity.')
L(2,'2.2.2 Highlights · Structural Change and Equity','Growth versus development; equal versus equitable',
'**Structural composition** means each sector’s share in GDP. **Structural change** means these shares change over time, normally with agriculture’s share declining and industry/services expanding. India experienced a particularly strong movement towards services. A smaller agricultural GDP share does not mean farming should be neglected.',
'**Growth** is a sustained rise in output. **Development** combines growth with equity and structural improvement. **Equal distribution** would give everyone the same income; **equitable distribution** permits differences related to skills and responsibilities while preventing excessive deprivation and ensuring basic opportunities.',
'**Inclusive growth** creates opportunities and distributes benefits widely. Modernisation was expected to support jobs through productivity → higher income → greater demand → more employment. Labour-saving technology can weaken that chain, producing jobless growth. Distinguish the intended complementarity from the possible trade-off.',kind='Box concept')
L(2,'2.3.1 Recap · 2.3.2 Land Reforms','Five land reforms and their purpose',
'The inherited problems were low productivity, low investment, scattered holdings, subsistence farming and exploitation. **Institutional reforms** changed agrarian ownership and relationships; **technical reforms** changed production methods. The basic institutional objective was equity through “land to the tiller”.',
'**Abolition of intermediaries:** restore cultivators’ rights, end exploitative rent collection and connect them directly with government. **Ceilings on holdings:** fix a maximum holding and redistribute surplus to small holders/landless workers. **Consolidation:** replace scattered plots with land in one place, reducing cultivation costs.',
'**Regulation of rent:** prevent excessive/illegal extraction; the chapter describes rent generally being limited to about one-third of crop value. **Cooperative farming:** pool resources to buy inputs cheaply, sell collectively and strengthen bargaining, without confusing cooperation with compulsory state ownership.')
L(2,'2.3.2 Hurdles in Implementation','Why land reforms achieved uneven results',
'**Eviction and evasion:** some landlords evicted tenants or presented themselves as actual cultivators to avoid transferring rights. **Incomplete coverage:** many landless agricultural labourers remained outside the benefits of ownership reforms.',
'**Court challenges and delay:** large owners challenged ceiling laws; the delay allowed them to transfer holdings to relatives and evade limits. Implementation also depended on political commitment and local administration. West Bengal and Kerala are highlighted as relatively successful examples.',
'The intended chain was secure rights → stronger incentive to improve land → higher productivity and equity. Weak implementation interrupted that chain. Therefore distinguish the purpose of the reform from its actual reach.')
L(2,'2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology','The Green Revolution input package',
'Green Revolution refers to a sharp increase in crop output associated with HYV technology, especially from the mid-1960s/1967–68. “Green” refers to crops and “revolution” to the substantial jump in production.',
'Its four textbook elements are **HYV seeds**, **chemical fertilisers**, **insecticides/pesticides for crop protection**, and **expanded irrigation**. High-yield seeds require complementary nutrients and reliable water; crop protection helps preserve the potential gain. Credit and extension services make the package accessible.',
'The package, rather than any single input, broke the earlier stagnation. Irrigation projects and plant-protection services supported adoption. A farmer receiving seeds alone may fail to benefit if water, inputs and finance are missing.')
L(2,'2.3.4 Two Distinct Phases of Green Revolution','The two phases and the role of government support',
'**Mid-1960s to mid-1970s:** gains were concentrated in wheat/rice-growing regions with adequate water. The supplied textbook names Punjab, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu. Adoption was initially easiest for prosperous farmers who could afford expensive complementary inputs.',
'**Mid-1970s to mid-1980s:** coverage widened across regions and crops. Low-interest loans and subsidised fertilisers/pesticides helped smaller farmers access the package. Government assistance therefore helped spread benefits beyond the initial adopters.',
'The second phase widened access but did not eliminate every regional and income inequality. In an answer on phases, include both the time periods and the change in geographical/social coverage—not just “first limited, second widespread”.')
L(2,'2.3.5 Gains of Green Revolution','The five gains of Green Revolution',
'**Spurt in crop productivity:** more output per hectare ended a long period of agricultural stagnation. **Substantial rise in acreage:** shorter crop duration and improved cultivation made multiple cropping more feasible; gross cropped area can rise even without an equal rise in physical land area.',
'**Shift from subsistence to commercial farming:** output beyond the farmer’s own needs created a marketable surplus. **Change in farmers’ outlook:** farming became a source of profit and reinvestment, rather than only survival.',
'**Self-sufficiency in foodgrains and buffer stocks:** larger supplies reduced import dependence and enabled reserves for lean seasons/emergencies. More food reaching the market can restrain prices and support consumers. These are distinct gains—do not collapse them into “production increased”.')
L(2,'2.3.5 Highlights · Marketable Surplus and Buffer Stock','Marketable surplus, fallowing, cropping and buffer stocks',
'**Marketable surplus** is output above the farmer’s on-farm requirements. Selling it provides cash for producer goods and household consumption. It indicates commercialisation, whereas total production includes what the family keeps.',
'**Fallowing** means leaving land uncultivated for a period. **Double/multiple cropping** uses land for more than one crop during a year; it raises gross cropped area and employment opportunities. Do not confuse physical land area with the sum of area cultivated across seasons.',
'**Buffer stock** is a reserve held for emergencies and seasonal shortages. The textbook links its introduction to the Fourth Plan. Foodgrain reserves support PDS distribution and respond to crop failure or natural disasters.',formula='Marketable surplus = Output − On-farm consumption',kind='Box concept')
L(2,'2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution','Four limitations and the equity problem',
'**Limited crops:** the strongest gains were concentrated in wheat and rice, without a comparable breakthrough in pulses and many commercial crops. **Uneven spread:** better-watered and supported states gained more; several eastern/central regions benefited less.',
'**Limited farming population:** the high input cost excluded many small and marginal farmers. **Economic divide:** prosperous farmers adopted more easily, so differences between rich and poor could widen despite subsidies. These headings concern crop coverage, geography, access and inequality respectively.',
'Excessive chemical and groundwater use also raises sustainability concerns discussed in rural development. Higher overall food output is a major achievement, but it does not prove that equity was achieved. A balanced appraisal gives the gains and limitations separately.')
L(2,'2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?','Arguments for and against agricultural subsidies',
'**A vice—huge fiscal burden:** support becomes costly if continued indiscriminately after a technology is established. **Misappropriated benefits:** input suppliers and prosperous farmers may capture benefits intended for poor cultivators. **Wasteful consumption:** artificially cheap water, electricity and chemicals encourage overuse and environmental harm.',
'**A virtue—risk and poverty:** farmers face uncertain harvests and many cannot afford the new input package. Subsidies encourage adoption and prevent technology being accessible only to rich farmers. Removing support can widen inequality.',
'The balanced conclusion is to improve targeting and use, rather than treating every subsidy as either necessary forever or wasteful by definition. Explain the mechanism after each heading: cheap water → little incentive to conserve → excessive extraction.')
L(2,'2.4.1 Public and Private Sectors','Why public-sector leadership was necessary',
'**Lack of capital:** the investment needed for basic/heavy industry was beyond the capacity of the few established private industrial houses. **Low inducement to invest:** low purchasing power and small markets reduced expected returns; infrastructure projects also had long gestation periods.',
'**Growth with social justice:** private profitability alone would not ensure regional balance or prevent concentration of wealth. Public investment could build capacity in neglected areas and supply essential infrastructure.',
'**Commanding heights** are strategic industries that support the rest of the economy—transport, communications, basic industry and defence. State control was intended to secure this foundation. The public sector was the leading investor, while private firms still participated in the mixed economy.')
L(2,'2.4.2 Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956','IPR 1956: classification, licensing and concessions',
'**Three-fold classification:** the first group was exclusively state-owned; the second allowed both sectors with the state taking the leading role; the third was left to regulated private enterprise. This established the state’s leading role and supported the Second Plan’s industrial strategy.',
'**Industrial licensing:** private firms needed a licence to establish or expand production. The state could direct scarce resources and encourage investment in backward regions. **Industrial concessions:** tax holidays, subsidised power and related incentives made investment in these regions more attractive.',
'The intention was coordinated industrialisation and regional equity. The later criticism was that excessive licensing also obstructed entry and competition. Keep the original purpose separate from weaknesses in implementation.')
L(2,'2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry','Small industries: three advantages and protection',
'The Karve Committee (1955) emphasised small-scale industry’s role in rural development. The chapter historically defines small industry through the investment permitted in plant/machinery; classification thresholds change, so the economic rationale matters more than treating an old limit as permanent.',
'**Labour-intensive/employment-oriented:** more workers can be employed with limited capital. **Locational flexibility/equality across regions:** small units can operate outside major industrial centres. **Small investment/equity across people:** entry is feasible for smaller entrepreneurs, reducing concentration of wealth.',
'Protection included reservation of products, lower excise duties and concessional credit. Weak finance, technology and marketing justified support, though prolonged protection could also reduce competitive pressure. Large industry built the basic industrial base; small industry pursued employment and decentralisation.')
L(2,'2.5 Trade Policy: Import Substitution','Import substitution versus export promotion',
'**Import substitution** saves foreign exchange by producing domestically what was previously imported. Scarce foreign exchange could then finance essential developmental imports such as machinery. **Export promotion** earns foreign exchange by making domestic products competitive abroad. They address the same scarcity from different sides.',
'Protection took two forms. **Tariffs** raise the price of imported goods through taxation. **Quotas** limit the quantity that may enter. Both reduce foreign competition and help domestic industries establish themselves.',
'The infant-industry rationale is that new domestic firms need time to develop. The limitation is that protection without pressure to improve can produce high costs, poor quality and weak international competitiveness. Saving foreign exchange is not the same as building export capacity.')
L(2,'2.6.1 Characteristic Features of Growth Strategy','The seven features of the 1950–1990 strategy',
'**Mixed economy and Five Year Plans** formed the overall framework. **Public enterprises** had the central industrial role; **private enterprises** played a regulated, secondary role in strategic industrialisation.',
'**Import substitution** guided production priorities to achieve self-reliance and conserve foreign exchange. **Protection from foreign competition** operated through duties and import quotas.',
'**Large-scale industry** built infrastructure and basic/heavy industry. **Small-scale industry** promoted employment, decentralisation and equity. Explain how each instrument related to a planning goal: for example, regional concessions supported equity, while machinery imports supported productive capacity.')
L(2,'2.6.2 Pros and Cons','Achievements and limitations of the planning strategy',
'**Achievements:** agriculture received a breakthrough through land/technical reforms; industrial output expanded; the industrial base diversified beyond cotton and jute into heavy industry and electronics; steel plants and other large projects created infrastructure; small units supported employment and regional spread.',
'**Excessive public-sector dominance:** government entered activities private firms could handle. **Public monopolies and losses:** weak accountability encouraged inefficiency, leakage and corruption. **Licence raj:** established firms could obtain licences to block competitors rather than expand production.',
'**Protection without competitiveness:** domestic growth did not always deliver internationally competitive prices/quality. **Inward-looking foreign-exchange policy:** import substitution received more emphasis than earning foreign exchange through exports. Agricultural productivity rose, but adequate non-farm jobs did not emerge to absorb surplus labour. The strategy broke stagnation while leaving important structural problems.')
L(2,'Ability Zone · Short Period Objectives','Plan-wise objectives and the annual-plan breaks',
'**First Plan, 1951–56:** agriculture and equitable distribution. **Second, 1956–61:** industrial production and heavy industry. **Third, 1961–66:** foodgrain self-sufficiency, employment and lower inequality. Three Annual Plans followed in 1966–69.',
'**Fourth, 1969–74:** growth and price stability. **Fifth, 1974–79:** living standards, especially weaker sections. An Annual Plan followed in 1979–80. **Sixth, 1980–85:** poverty removal, reduced inequality and infrastructure. **Seventh, 1985–90:** jobs and agricultural productivity. Two Annual Plans followed in 1990–92.',
'**Eighth, 1992–97:** fuller manpower use, elementary education and infrastructure. **Ninth, 1997–2002:** agriculture/rural development, stable growth and population control. **Tenth, 2002–07:** quality of life, health, education and consumption. **Eleventh, 2007–12:** inclusive growth, education/health quality, a second agricultural push, quality jobs and environment. **Twelfth, 2012–17:** faster, sustainable and more inclusive growth. These are the extension table’s short-period priorities; the four common goals apply across plans.',kind='Ability Zone')
L(2,'Ability Zone · NITI Aayog','NITI Aayog: five functions',
'NITI Aayog—the National Institution for Transforming India—was established on 1 January 2015, replacing the Planning Commission. It is the government’s policy think tank, chaired by the Prime Minister.',
'**Policy formulation:** develop long-term strategies. **Monitoring and evaluation:** assess implementation and offer expert advice. **Cooperative and competitive federalism:** give Centre/states a common platform and encourage improvement through comparative performance.',
'**Technology upgradation and capacity building:** improve institutional and technical capability. **Promoting equity:** support inclusive growth so benefits reach all sections. Cooperative federalism means working together; competitive federalism means learning from comparison and striving to perform better.',kind='Ability Zone')
L(2,'Ability Zone · Distribution and Interventionist State','Why growth did not automatically solve poverty',
'An **interventionist state** regulates markets and directly supports provision so essential goods reach poorer sections. Markets allocate according to purchasing power, which is different from allocating according to need.',
'The textbook’s starvation-with-food-stocks example shows the distribution problem: sufficient food production can coexist with hunger when households lack income or PDS delivery fails. The same distinction applies to income, consumption expenditure, land and industrial wealth: an increase in totals does not establish equitable ownership or access.',
'Land reforms improved some cultivators’ rights, but shrinking/fragmented holdings constrained efficiency; industrial assets could remain concentrated in large houses. When interpreting poverty or tax data, distinguish a percentage from an absolute number—a falling share can coexist with a rising count if the population grows enough.',kind='Ability Zone')
L(2,'Ability Zone · Paradox of Saving and Investment','Why higher investment need not produce rapid growth',
'The chapter asks why saving and investment could rise while output growth remained slow. **High capital-output ratio:** a larger amount of capital was needed for each extra unit of output. **Inventory rather than fixed-capital formation:** foodgrain stocks are investment in accounting terms but do not create machinery or productive capacity in the same way.',
'**Low-productivity investment:** resources placed in traditional activities using backward technology generated relatively low returns. The issue is therefore the composition and efficiency of investment, not only its quantity.',
'This is the planning chapter’s saving–investment paradox. Do not confuse it with Macro’s paradox of thrift, where a collective attempt to save more can reduce demand and income.',kind='Ability Zone')
# CHAPTER 3 — New Economic Policy, mechanisms, appraisal and boxes.
L(3,'3.1 Background of Economic Reforms','Why reforms became necessary in 1991',
'The earlier mixed-economy strategy built an industrial base, but restrictions, inefficient public enterprises and weak competition increasingly constrained productivity. Government expenditure exceeded revenue, public borrowing rose, and debt servicing absorbed resources. Inflation raised living costs and production costs.',
'The immediate trigger was a **balance-of-payments crisis**: foreign-exchange reserves were insufficient for more than about two weeks of imports, making essential imports and external payments difficult. India approached the World Bank and IMF for assistance of approximately $7 billion. Assistance came with conditions promoting reforms and greater participation in world markets.',
'The **New Economic Policy** sought a more competitive economy through liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation. Distinguish the immediate foreign-exchange crisis from the deeper fiscal and structural problems that made the economy vulnerable.')
L(3,'3.2 Liberalisation','Meaning and purpose of liberalisation',
'**Liberalisation** means removing unnecessary government controls and restrictions so enterprises have greater freedom to make economic decisions. Earlier controls included industrial licensing, restrictions on expansion, import licensing, foreign-exchange controls and regulated prices or interest rates.',
'The expectation was that competition, market signals and freedom to invest would improve efficiency, quality and productivity. Reforms covered industry, finance, taxation, foreign exchange, trade and investment. Greater freedom does not mean that safety standards, banking supervision or every form of government intervention disappears.',
'**Laissez-faire**, discussed in the box, is the stronger idea of minimal state interference, with the state largely protecting life, property and contracts. Liberalisation in India involves reducing controls within a regulated economy, rather than adopting that extreme position.',kind='Concept and box')
L(3,'3.2.1 Industrial Sector Reforms','Five changes in industrial regulation',
'**Abolition of industrial licensing:** most industries could begin production without obtaining an industrial licence. The textbook lists alcohol, cigarettes, defence equipment, industrial explosives and dangerous chemicals as historical exceptions. These illustrate retained controls in sensitive activities, rather than a permanent list of today’s licensing rules.',
'**Contraction of public-sector reservation:** more activities were opened to private enterprise; the chapter traces reservation from 17 industries to eight and subsequently three in its historical account. **De-reservation of small-scale industries:** many goods earlier reserved for small producers could be produced by larger firms.',
'**Freedom to expand capacity:** firms obtained greater freedom to determine their scale of production according to market demand. **Freedom to import capital goods and technology:** easier access to machinery and international technology agreements supported modernisation. Explain the control removed and its intended effect, rather than merely writing “less regulation”.')
L(3,'3.2.2 Financial Sector Reforms','From financial controller to facilitator',
'The financial sector includes banks, stock exchanges and other institutions that mobilise savings and provide funds. Reforms aimed to shift the RBI’s role from detailed control towards facilitating efficient financial activity while retaining prudential supervision and financial stability.',
'**Greater competition:** private Indian banks and foreign banks were allowed greater participation. **Operational freedom:** banks obtained more scope to decide lending and deposit interest rates and open branches subject to applicable conditions. **Access to funds:** financial institutions could raise funds more freely in domestic and overseas markets.',
'**Foreign institutional investors:** institutions such as mutual funds, pension funds and merchant bankers gained access to Indian financial markets. Greater foreign participation expanded the potential supply of finance. The chapter’s particular ownership ceilings describe its policy period; the lasting concept is reduced entry restrictions combined with continued regulation.')
L(3,'3.2.2 Financial Reforms · Demonetisation','Demonetisation: intended gains and immediate costs',
'On **8 November 2016**, the existing ₹500 and ₹1,000 notes ceased to be legal tender under the announced demonetisation programme. People were given a specified period to deposit or exchange eligible notes, and new notes were introduced. Demonetisation changes the legal-tender status of currency; it is different from inflation reducing currency’s purchasing power.',
'**Intended gains:** expose unaccounted money and reduce the shadow economy; widen the tax base and tax revenue; increase bank deposits and funds available for lending; disrupt counterfeit currency and terror financing; encourage digital payments; and promote formal banking and financial inclusion. These are policy objectives and possible channels, not proof that every objective was fully achieved.',
'**Immediate costs:** cash shortages disrupted everyday transactions; production and trade suffered, especially in the cash-dependent informal sector; workers lost employment or earnings; long queues imposed time and inconvenience; and weaker consumption and investment slowed activity. An appraisal should explain both the intended mechanism and these adjustment costs.',kind='Important box')
L(3,'3.2.3 Tax Reforms','Direct taxes, indirect taxes and tax reform',
'**Fiscal policy** concerns government revenue and expenditure. A **direct tax** is paid by the person on whom its legal burden falls, as with income tax; its burden is not normally shifted to another person. An **indirect tax**, such as GST or customs duty, can be passed on through prices.',
'Tax reforms sought to rationalise rates, simplify procedures and improve compliance. Very high rates and complicated rules can encourage evasion and discourage enterprise. Lower or more reasonable rates, fewer complications and a wider tax base were expected to encourage voluntary payment and improve revenue collection.',
'The objective is not simply “cut every tax”. A sound explanation connects reasonable rates and simpler administration to incentives, compliance, coverage and the government’s ability to finance development.')
L(3,'3.2.3 Tax Reforms · GST','GST: value addition, advantages and limitations',
'**Goods and Services Tax**, introduced on **1 July 2017**, brought many indirect taxes into a common framework. **Supply-based tax:** liability is linked to the supply of goods and services. **Value addition:** tax is intended to apply to the value added at successive stages. **Input-tax credit:** eligible tax already paid on inputs can be credited against output-tax liability, reducing tax-on-tax or cascading.',
'**Advantages:** a simpler and more uniform indirect-tax system; a more integrated national market; greater transparency through recorded transactions; and potential improvement in compliance and revenue, with less scope for unrecorded business. A common framework does not mean one identical rate for every product.',
'**Limitations:** some products remain outside GST’s ordinary coverage, including alcoholic liquor for human consumption and specified petroleum products; electricity is also outside GST. Firms can face compliance burdens and uncertainty when procedures or rates change. Learn the mechanism and these strengths and constraints rather than treating a changing rate table as the definition.',kind='Important box')
L(3,'3.2.4 Foreign Exchange Reforms','Devaluation and market-based exchange rates',
'In 1991, the rupee was devalued to address the external-payments crisis. A lower external value makes Indian exports cheaper to foreign buyers and imports more expensive in rupees, potentially improving export competitiveness and restraining imports. The textbook describes the two-step adjustment in July 1991.',
'Exchange-rate reforms subsequently gave market demand and supply a greater role in determining the rupee’s value. This facilitated international transactions and linked India more closely with world markets. **Devaluation** is an official reduction under a fixed or administered arrangement; **depreciation** is a fall associated with market forces.',
'The effect on the trade balance is not automatic: buyers must respond to price changes, and essential imports may remain necessary. The intended channel is the change in the relative prices of exports and imports.')
L(3,'3.2.5 Trade and Investment Policy Reforms','Opening trade and investment',
'**Removal of quantitative restrictions:** limits on the amount of many imports were reduced or removed. **Reduction of tariffs:** lower import duties reduced protection and exposed domestic producers to competition. **Relaxation of import licensing:** most imports no longer required the earlier licence system, with sensitive health, safety and environmental restrictions retained.',
'**Removal or reduction of export duties and restrictions:** these measures aimed to encourage exports and improve international competitiveness. Easier foreign investment and access to imported inputs, machinery and technology were intended to improve production quality and efficiency.',
'Tariffs change the price of imports; quotas restrict their quantity. For an exam answer, keep that distinction explicit and explain why trade reform can benefit consumers while placing adjustment pressure on protected domestic firms.')
L(3,'3.3 Privatisation','Privatisation, disinvestment and the public-sector journey',
'**Privatisation** means reducing or withdrawing government ownership or management of an enterprise. It may involve transfer of management, sale of part of the government’s equity, or complete sale to private owners. **Disinvestment** means selling government shareholdings: a minority share sale need not transfer management or make an enterprise fully private.',
'Public enterprises originally helped diversify production, build basic industries and infrastructure, and address activities private capital could not adequately undertake. Over time, some faced inefficiency, losses, excessive interference, leakages, pilferage and corruption. Reforms aimed to reduce this burden and improve accountability and performance.',
'**Navratna, Maharatna and Miniratna** status gives selected public enterprises greater managerial and financial autonomy. Autonomy can improve investment decisions and competitiveness while government ownership continues. These categories are therefore not themselves privatisation, and the status of individual firms can change.',kind='Concept and box')
L(3,'3.3 Privatisation · Appraisal','Benefits and social costs of privatisation',
'**Efficiency:** owners with a direct financial stake have an incentive to control costs and improve output. **Competition and modernisation:** pressure to survive can encourage better technology and management. **Diversification and reinvestment:** commercially viable firms can develop new products and reinvest profits. **Consumer choice:** competing firms may offer better quality and a wider range of goods.',
'**Social-interest concern:** commercial profitability may receive priority over affordable essential services or employment. **Weak purchasing power:** poorer groups may be unable to buy services even when the market supplies them efficiently. Regulation and public support remain important where social benefits exceed private returns.',
'The **Sircilla powerloom case** connects higher electricity charges and production costs to reduced output, lower wages and distress among weavers. Use the chain “input cost → viability → employment and earnings” to explain why a reform’s distributional effects matter, rather than assuming either all private enterprise or all public enterprise is inherently efficient.',kind='Concept and case')
L(3,'3.4 Globalisation','Globalisation and its five policy supports',
'**Globalisation** is the increasing integration and interdependence of economies through flows of goods, services, capital, technology and expertise. Liberalisation and privatisation created conditions for greater integration; globalisation describes the resulting connections with the world economy.',
'**Encouragement of foreign investment:** relaxation of equity restrictions and a more facilitative foreign-exchange framework, including FEMA. **Rupee convertibility for current transactions:** easier conversion for trade in goods and services, interest, dividends and remittances; this is distinct from unrestricted capital-account convertibility.',
'**Long-term trade policy:** a more predictable outward-looking framework. **Reduction in tariffs:** lower import duties. **Reduction in quantitative restrictions:** removal of many import quantity controls, with the chapter highlighting 2001 in the historical reform sequence. These measures increased competition and international access; they did not abolish every restriction.')
L(3,'3.4 Globalisation · Outsourcing','Outsourcing: why India became a service destination',
'**Outsourcing** occurs when an organisation contracts work to an outside provider instead of performing it internally. International outsourcing places that work in another country. Examples include call centres, record keeping, accountancy, banking support, music and film editing, medical transcription, clinical services and online teaching.',
'Improved information and communication technology allows work and results to move rapidly across borders. India’s relatively low labour costs and large pool of skilled, English-speaking workers made it attractive for many service activities. Firms seek cost savings and expertise; Indian workers and firms can gain income, employment and export opportunities.',
'The benefit depends on skills and access to infrastructure, so it may be concentrated in particular workers and cities. Outsourcing is one channel of globalisation, not a synonym for every foreign investment or every export.',kind='Important box')
L(3,'3.4 Globalisation · WTO','WTO: role, expected benefits and unequal access',
'The **World Trade Organization** began on **1 January 1995**, succeeding the GATT framework established in 1948. The Marrakesh agreement associated with its creation involved 123 participating countries. Its purpose is a rules-based international trading system.',
'**Five roles:** promote freer trade; discourage arbitrary restrictions through common rules; improve competition and market access; provide a framework for trade agreements and their implementation; and expand markets so resources can be used more efficiently. A **bilateral** agreement involves two countries; a **multilateral** agreement involves several.',
'**India’s expected gains:** more export opportunities; textile employment and exports following removal of Multi-Fibre Arrangement quotas; and agricultural opportunities if developed countries reduced subsidies and barriers. **Concern:** implementation can be unequal—richer countries may retain support or barriers, and capital often moves more freely than labour. Expected gains should be distinguished from guaranteed gains.')
L(3,'3.4 Ability Zone · Generations of Reform','First-generation and second-generation reforms',
'**First-generation reforms** mainly changed administrative and executive controls, such as licensing, trade restrictions and investment approvals. These could often be implemented through government policy decisions.',
'**Second-generation reforms** seek deeper institutional changes, including legal frameworks and areas such as labour, governance and public services. They may require legislation, coordination and broader political agreement, making implementation more difficult or slower.',
'The distinction explains why announcing market-opening measures is easier than ensuring effective institutions, skills, infrastructure and social protection. Those complementary conditions affect whether the initial reforms deliver broad benefits.',kind='Ability Zone')
L(3,'3.5 Appraisal · Stabilisation and Structural Adjustment','Two different jobs of the reform programme',
'**Stabilisation measures** address immediate macroeconomic imbalances: the balance-of-payments crisis, inflation and unsustainable fiscal deficits. They aim to restore the economy’s ability to make payments and maintain stability in the short run.',
'**Structural adjustment measures** change how the economy operates over the longer run: improving efficiency, increasing competition, reducing restrictive controls and encouraging investment and modernisation. Industrial, financial and trade reforms belong to this broader restructuring.',
'The two are related but different. Securing foreign exchange can ease an immediate crisis without by itself making firms efficient; improving competition can raise long-term productivity while imposing short-run adjustment costs.')
L(3,'3.5.1 Positive Impact of LPG Reforms','Nine favourable outcomes: growth, stability and choice',
'**Vibrant economy:** greater openness and competition encouraged economic activity. **Stimulant to industrial production:** access to capital, technology and markets improved opportunities for expansion and modernisation. **Check on fiscal deficit:** fiscal discipline and reform aimed to reduce the government’s imbalance. **Larger foreign-exchange reserves:** improved external inflows strengthened the payments position.',
'**Check on inflation:** stabilisation and improved supplies helped contain price pressures in parts of the reform period. **Consumer sovereignty:** wider choice and competition increased the importance of consumer preferences. **Private and foreign investment:** a more liberal framework encouraged new capital and enterprise.',
'**Emerging economic power:** faster growth and international integration increased India’s economic significance. **From monopoly to competition:** reduced protection and entry barriers challenged established producers. These are the chapter’s favourable appraisal headings; performance varied by sector and year, so a rise in investment or reserves alone does not prove that every social objective was met.')
L(3,'3.5 Appraisal · FDI and FII','FDI versus foreign institutional investment',
'**Foreign direct investment (FDI)** involves a lasting business interest and a significant role in ownership or management—for example, establishing a factory or acquiring a controlling business stake. It can bring technology, management and production capacity as well as finance.',
'**Foreign institutional investment (FII)** generally involves institutional investment in financial securities such as shares and bonds without taking managerial control of the enterprise. It supplies portfolio finance and can be more readily withdrawn as investors change their preferences.',
'Both are foreign investment, but their relationship with production, control and financial-market volatility differs. Do not identify every purchase of an Indian share by a foreign institution as the establishment of a foreign-controlled business.',kind='Important box')
L(3,'3.5.2 Negative Impact · Agriculture and Industry','Why agriculture and industry did not benefit equally',
'**Neglect of agriculture:** lower public investment in irrigation, power, roads and research constrained productivity; reduced subsidies could raise input costs; lower import duties exposed farmers to foreign competition; and a shift towards export-oriented cash crops could create risks for food production. Agricultural weakness also hurts industry by reducing raw-material supply and rural demand.',
'**Industrial slowdown and uneven competition:** cheaper imports could displace domestic production, while smaller firms struggled to upgrade technology. Indian exporters could still face barriers in richer countries, creating an uneven playing field. Infrastructure and finance constraints limited the ability to respond to competition.',
'**Unemployment or jobless growth:** capital-intensive technology can expand output without proportionate employment. Workers displaced from protected or public enterprises may lack the skills required in expanding sectors. Explain why higher GDP does not automatically mean enough suitable jobs.')
L(3,'3.5.2 Negative Impact · Fiscal and Distributional Concerns','The remaining six criticisms of reforms',
'**Disinvestment:** selling valuable public assets cheaply or using proceeds mainly for routine expenditure can weaken long-term public wealth. **Fiscal policy:** lower customs duties and tax concessions can constrain revenue and funds for social expenditure if wider activity and compliance do not compensate.',
'**Lopsided growth:** rapid expansion of services, particularly IT, can coexist with weak agriculture and manufacturing. **Urban concentration:** firms prefer cities with better infrastructure and skilled labour, widening regional and rural–urban gaps. **Economic colonialism:** excessive dependence on foreign firms, technology or markets can reduce domestic economic autonomy.',
'**Consumerism:** advertising and demonstration effects may encourage consumption beyond means and weaken saving priorities. The related **dualism** box describes the coexistence of modern, high-income activities and traditional, low-productivity ones. A balanced policy encourages useful investment and competition while supporting infrastructure, domestic capabilities, weaker sections and strategic needs.',kind='Appraisal and box')
# CHAPTER 4 — Human capital, every source, education and policy boxes.
L(4,'4.1 Human Capital and Human Capital Formation','Human resources, human capital and formation',
'People become an economic resource through their capacity to work, learn and organise production. **Human capital** is the stock of knowledge, skills, abilities and health embodied in people that increases their productive capacity. **Human capital formation** is the process of adding to this stock through education, training, health and related investments.',
'The stock–flow distinction matters: a person’s existing skills are human capital; acquiring additional skills during a period is human capital formation. Expenditure is an investment when it develops capabilities that generate benefits over time, rather than merely meeting a current need.',
'Human capital formation can raise earnings and national output, but its benefits also include improved judgement, adaptability and participation. The textbook’s definition associated with Meier emphasises developing people’s skills and capabilities for productive activity.')
L(4,'4.1 Human Capital and Human Development','Productive capacity versus human well-being',
'**Human capital** treats education and health as means of raising productivity and income. **Human development** treats people’s well-being, capabilities and freedom as valuable ends in themselves. Education and good health therefore matter even when their immediate contribution to market output is small.',
'An educated, healthy worker may produce more; that is the human-capital argument. A person also deserves opportunities to learn and live a healthy life; that is the human-development argument. The approaches overlap, but the second has a broader purpose.',
'For a distinction question, compare the objective, the role of education and health, and the criterion of success: higher productive capacity and earnings versus a better and more capable human life.')
L(4,'4.1 Physical Capital versus Human Capital','Seven bases of distinction',
'**Formation:** physical capital is created using material inputs; human capital develops through education, health and training. **Decision making:** entrepreneurs usually make conscious investment decisions about machinery, whereas human-capital decisions may initially be made by parents, institutions or government on a person’s behalf.',
'**Tangibility:** machines and buildings are tangible; skills and knowledge are embodied capabilities. **Separability:** a machine can be sold separately from its owner, but a person’s skills cannot be separated from that person. **Mobility:** both can move, but human movement is also constrained by nationality, culture, family and immigration rules.',
'**Depreciation:** physical capital wears out; human capabilities can deteriorate through ageing, poor health or obsolete knowledge, making retraining important. **Benefits:** physical investment mainly generates private returns, while education and health also produce substantial social benefits. Use these as comparison bases, not as a claim that physical investment never benefits society.')
L(4,'4.2 Sources of Human Capital Formation','Education and health as investments',
'**Expenditure on education** develops knowledge and skills, improves employability and productivity, and can generate higher lifetime earnings. Its costs include fees and materials as well as the earnings forgone while studying. Benefits arrive over time, so education is treated as an investment in people.',
'**Expenditure on health** improves the ability to work regularly and effectively. The textbook includes **preventive medicine** such as vaccination, **curative medicine** for treatment, **social medicine** through health awareness, and **sanitation** through safe water, hygiene and waste disposal.',
'Healthy people lose fewer working days, learn more effectively and can use their skills better. Education and health reinforce each other: knowledge improves health choices, while health improves learning and attendance.')
L(4,'4.2 Sources · On-the-job Training and Adult Education','Learning beyond school',
'**On-the-job training** builds skills while a person is employed. It may take place inside the firm under experienced workers or through an external training institution. Firms incur training costs because better skills can improve quality, efficiency and output; arrangements encouraging workers to remain help firms recover that investment.',
'**Adult education** gives people opportunities to gain literacy, knowledge and practical capabilities after the usual school age. Government agencies and voluntary organisations can provide such programmes, enabling people excluded from initial schooling to participate more effectively in work and society.',
'Training is especially important when technology changes. It maintains the value of existing human capital and addresses the difference between possessing a qualification and possessing the particular skills a job requires.')
L(4,'4.2 Sources · Migration and Information','Why moving and searching can build productive capacity',
'**Migration** may be internal or international. People move to obtain better employment and earnings or to use their skills more effectively. Costs include travel, higher living expenses and the psychological cost of separation from family and familiar surroundings; the decision compares these with expected future benefits.',
'**Information** about educational institutions, training, vacancies, wages and working conditions helps people choose where to invest in skills and where to work. Collecting it takes time and money, but can reduce mismatch and improve returns to human capabilities.',
'These sources do not necessarily create a new skill immediately. They improve the acquisition, allocation or use of human capital—an important reason the textbook includes them alongside education, health and training.')
L(4,'4.3 Human Capital Formation and Economic Growth','Three ways human capital supports development',
'**Change in emotional and material environment:** education broadens aspirations, reasoning and attitudes towards change, while a skilled workforce improves the material conditions of production. People become better able to adopt new methods and pursue improved living standards.',
'**Higher productivity of physical capital:** trained and healthy workers use equipment efficiently, reduce waste and develop innovations and inventions. The same machinery can yield different output depending on the capabilities of those using it.',
'**Greater participation and equality:** wider access to capabilities enables more people, including disadvantaged groups and women, to contribute to development and share its benefits. The Seventh Five-Year Plan recognised human resources as central to development. The relationship is two-way: human capital promotes growth, and higher income finances further education and health.')
L(4,'4.3 Ability Zone · Measuring Human Capital','Why years of schooling do not tell the whole story',
'Indicators such as enrolment, years of schooling and teacher–pupil ratios measure access or inputs, but do not completely capture learning quality. Similarly, health expenditure does not automatically measure how healthy people are or how effectively healthcare is delivered.',
'Countries with similar measured education or health can have different growth rates because infrastructure, institutions, technology and employment opportunities also matter. This does not show that human capital is unimportant; it shows that measurement is incomplete and growth has several causes.',
'Use the full chain: investment must improve actual capabilities, those capabilities must be used productively, and complementary conditions must support their use.',kind='Ability Zone')
L(4,'4.4 Problems of Human Capital Formation','Five obstacles in India',
'**Rising population:** resources must serve more people, limiting improvements per person when investment does not keep pace. **Brain drain:** skilled people trained using domestic resources may migrate abroad, reducing the immediate domestic return on that investment.',
'**Deficient manpower planning:** education and training may not match the number and type of jobs available, producing skill shortages alongside educated unemployment. **Inadequate on-the-job training:** especially in traditional and small activities, workers may have few opportunities to update practical skills.',
'**Low academic standards:** weak teaching, facilities or assessment can produce qualifications without adequate learning. The answer is therefore not merely “spend more”: expand access, improve quality, align training with opportunities and make effective use of existing skills.')
L(4,'4.5 Education · Meaning and Importance','Literacy, education and seven benefits',
'**Literacy** is the ability to read and write with understanding; **education** is the broader development of knowledge, reasoning, skills and personality. A literate person is not necessarily well educated. Education is commonly organised into primary, secondary and tertiary levels.',
'**Responsible citizens:** informed people make better social and civic choices. **Science and technology:** education supports invention and adoption of improved methods. **Efficient use of resources:** knowledge reduces waste and improves decisions. **Wider mental horizon:** it develops reasoning and openness to new ideas.',
'**Participation in growth:** skills allow people to enter productive activities. **Higher cultural standards:** education develops understanding of social and cultural life. **Human personality and empowerment:** it builds confidence, independence and agency, particularly for women and disadvantaged groups. These social gains extend beyond the student’s own earnings.')
L(4,'4.5 Government Intervention in Education and Health','Four reasons for public involvement',
'**Large investment requirements:** institutions, teachers and facilities need substantial resources and long-term commitment. **Affordability:** private providers charging to recover costs can exclude poorer families, even when educating them would benefit society.',
'**Standards and information:** students and patients cannot always judge quality in advance; regulation and reliable standards help protect them. **Rights and social responsibility:** access to basic education and healthcare should not depend entirely on purchasing power.',
'Government therefore finances provision, operates institutions, regulates quality and supports access. NCERT supports school curriculum and educational materials, UGC has roles in higher education, and AICTE concerns technical education. ICMR supports medical research. Different bodies serve different functions; do not treat them as interchangeable.')
L(4,'4.5 Public Expenditure and Educational Policy','How to interpret expenditure and the 6% recommendation',
'Educational expenditure can be expressed as a **share of total government expenditure**, showing its budget priority, or as a **share of GDP**, showing public resources committed relative to the economy’s size. These denominators answer different questions.',
'The textbook reports a rise from 7.92% to 16.54% of government expenditure and from 0.64% to 4.47% of GDP between 1952 and 2020. These are historical public-expenditure figures, not today’s rates and not total public-plus-private spending. The Education Commission of 1964–66 recommended expenditure of around **6% of GDP**.',
'The Tapas Majumdar Committee in 1999 estimated substantial additional resources—about ₹1.37 lakh crore over its proposed programme—for universal elementary education. The **Right to Education Act, 2009** provides for free and compulsory education for children aged **6–14**. The chapter also discusses education cess as a financing measure; distinguish historical financing arrangements from the enduring right and spending objective.',kind='Concept and policy box')
L(4,'4.5 Educational Expenditure · Distribution','Elementary, higher and state-level spending',
'Elementary education receives a large absolute share because it serves many children, while expenditure **per student** is generally higher in tertiary education. A larger total allocation and a larger per-student allocation are not the same thing.',
'The lesson is to expand resources across levels, rather than finance basic education only by weakening higher education. Higher education develops teachers, researchers, professionals and technical capabilities needed throughout the system. States differ greatly in resources, enrolment needs and spending per person; state governments finance much of public education.',
'When comparing states, specify the year, the denominator and what is included. Higher spending alone does not establish better outcomes unless facilities, teaching quality, attendance and learning also improve.')
L(4,'4.5 Achievements and Knowledge Economy','Progress, knowledge economy and NEP 2020',
'India has expanded educational institutions, enrolment and literacy since independence. The textbook compares literacy of **18.33% in 1951** with **74.04% in 2011**. These census figures demonstrate long-run progress, while also showing that universal literacy had not yet been achieved.',
'A **knowledge economy** relies heavily on the creation, communication and productive use of knowledge. IT, digital networks and access to information can improve productivity, but require both access and quality skills. A large number of graduates is not sufficient if their learning does not match changing work.',
'The **National Education Policy 2020** emphasises broader and multidisciplinary learning. Areas such as artificial intelligence and big data demand combinations of mathematical, scientific, technical and communication skills. The challenge is to connect expanded educational access with strong learning and adaptability.',kind='Concept and box')
L(4,'4.5 Problems and Challenges in Education','Six continuing gaps and the poverty cycle',
'**Large number of illiterates:** progress in the literacy rate still leaves many people without basic capabilities. **Inadequate vocational orientation:** degree-focused education and preference for white-collar work can coexist with shortages of practical skills. **Gender bias:** girls may face early withdrawal, household responsibilities and unequal opportunities.',
'**Low rural access:** distance, inadequate facilities and teacher shortages restrict learning. **Expensive private education:** unequal purchasing power creates unequal educational opportunities. **Inadequate public expenditure:** weak funding limits institutions, staff and quality.',
'The education pyramid narrows sharply towards higher levels. Poverty can force children out of school, while poor education limits earnings and reproduces poverty. Formal entitlement under RTE is not enough if documentation barriers, indirect costs, poor facilities and weak teaching prevent meaningful participation. The remedy combines access, retention, quality and practical relevance.')
# CHAPTER 5 — Rural Development.
L(5,'5.1 Rural Development','Meaning, importance and the rural development agenda',
'**Rural development** is a comprehensive action plan to improve rural economic and social life. It integrates productive employment, income, education, health, infrastructure and environmental sustainability. Agriculture is central, but rural development is broader than increasing crop output.',
'A large population depends on rural activities, and poverty and low productivity remain concentrated there. Slower agricultural growth and limited non-farm opportunities can widen rural–urban differences even when national GDP rises. Development must improve crop productivity and profitability while creating opportunities outside cultivation.',
'**Lingering challenges:** adequate credit and effective agricultural marketing. **Emerging challenges:** diversification of productive activities and sustainable practices such as organic farming. Roads, power, irrigation, research, education, healthcare and skills support all these efforts.')
L(5,'5.2 Rural Credit · Need and Types','Why farmers borrow and the three credit periods',
'Farmers incur costs before receiving harvest income. Small holdings and low retained surplus make it difficult to finance seeds, fertilisers, electricity and labour out of savings. Credit bridges the gap between sowing and sale and finances lasting improvements.',
'**Short-term credit, roughly 6–12 months:** seasonal inputs and recurring expenses. **Medium-term credit, about 1–5 years:** machinery, fencing and improvement of wells. **Long-term credit, about 5–20 years:** land purchase and permanent improvements. The purpose and repayment period should match the investment’s benefits.',
'**Productive credit** supports income-generating activity; **consumption or other non-productive credit** meets household and social expenditure. Borrowing for essential consumption may still be necessary, but repayment is difficult when it does not create additional income.',formula='Credit need = expenditure before harvest + investment needs − available savings')
L(5,'5.2 Evolution of Rural Credit','From moneylenders to social banking',
'Moneylenders, traders and commission agents traditionally dominated rural credit. High interest, manipulation of accounts, tied sales and weak bargaining power could leave farmers in a persistent debt trap. Their accessibility did not ensure fair terms.',
'**Bank nationalisation in 1969** promoted social banking and wider rural access. The Green Revolution increased the need for credit because purchased inputs and commercial production required more working capital. The aim was to provide timely, affordable institutional finance instead of dependence on exploitative arrangements.',
'The **multi-agency approach** uses government, cooperative institutions, commercial banks, regional rural banks and land-development banks, supported by NABARD and the RBI. These institutions have complementary roles rather than identical functions.')
L(5,'5.2 Institutional Sources of Rural Credit','Cooperatives, commercial banks, RRBs and land-development banks',
'**Cooperative credit societies** pool resources and provide accessible, timely credit. Their objectives include reducing moneylender dependence, spreading finance across regions and supporting weaker or neglected areas; some also provide agricultural advice and related assistance.',
'**Commercial banks**, including SBI established in 1955 and banks brought under public ownership in 1969, finance agriculture directly and through cooperative arrangements. **Regional rural banks** focus on rural and relatively backward areas, particularly smaller farmers and weaker sections.',
'**Land-development banks** mainly provide longer-term finance for land and permanent agricultural improvements. Availability alone is insufficient: loan timing, terms, accessibility and suitability for small borrowers determine whether credit helps production.')
L(5,'5.2 NABARD and RBI','Apex support and coordination of rural finance',
'**NABARD**, established in **1982**, is the apex institution supporting agricultural and rural development finance. Its main roles are providing or refinancing funds, strengthening and restructuring the credit-delivery system, training and coordinating institutions, and monitoring projects supported through its refinancing.',
'The **RBI’s supporting functions** in the textbook include short-term refinancing; developing cooperative credit; supporting funds for longer-term rural finance; helping state participation in cooperative institutions; training personnel; undertaking rural-credit surveys; encouraging rural branch expansion; and providing remittance facilities and guidance.',
'NABARD and the RBI strengthen the system behind the lender. A farmer’s ordinary loan may come from a bank or cooperative even when an apex institution supplies refinancing or institutional support.')
L(5,'5.2 Microcredit and Self-Help Groups','How SHGs reach borrowers without collateral',
'**Self-help groups (SHGs)** bring members, often poor rural women, together to save small amounts regularly. Pooled savings and bank linkages provide small loans for household needs and self-employment. **Microcredit** means credit in small amounts suited to borrowers poorly served by conventional banking.',
'Members’ mutual knowledge and group responsibility can substitute for conventional collateral, with comparatively simple formalities and manageable interest. Activities such as livestock rearing, food processing and small enterprises can increase income, confidence and financial independence.',
'The **Kudumbashree example in Kerala** illustrates women’s savings and group-based enterprise. The value is not just the size of a loan: regular saving, collective decision making and access to institutions can strengthen bargaining power and participation.',kind='Concept and box')
L(5,'5.2 Financial Inclusion · KCC and Jan Dhan','Timely farm credit and access to banking',
'The **Kisan Credit Card (KCC)** provides a convenient credit arrangement for eligible agricultural needs, with an assessed limit and access when funds are required. It reduces the need to complete a fresh full loan process for every seasonal expense.',
'**Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana**, launched in **2014**, expands access to bank accounts and associated financial services. The chapter discusses basic accounts, eligible overdraft and insurance facilities, and direct receipt of wages, pensions or benefits into accounts.',
'Financial inclusion involves usable services, not just opening an account. Affordable borrowing, regular saving, reliable payments and understanding how to use facilities determine whether access improves a household’s position.',kind='Important box')
L(5,'5.2 Appraisal of Rural Banking','Achievements, weaknesses and the way forward',
'**Achievements:** wider organised credit, rural branch expansion and NABARD support reduced some dependence on moneylenders. Credit helped farmers buy modern inputs and move towards commercial production.',
'**Weaknesses:** collateral and formalities can exclude poorer borrowers; low saving and weak deposit mobilisation constrain lending; overdue loans weaken institutions; and the social priority of rural lending can weaken under commercial pressure. Distinguish genuine inability to repay after crop failure from wilful default.',
'Credit should support viable production, with suitable repayment terms, insurance, information and marketing support. Repeated lending without a realistic income source does not remove poverty; equally, treating every poor borrower as uncreditworthy excludes the people social banking is meant to serve.')
L(5,'5.2 Agricultural Marketing','Marketing is a chain, not just a sale',
'**Agricultural marketing** includes assembling produce, processing, grading, packaging, storing, transporting, selling and distributing it. A farmer’s final return depends on this entire chain, not only on the price quoted at the farm gate.',
'Farmers may face faulty weights, manipulated accounts, excessive commissions, inadequate market information and poor transport or storage. Debt can tie them to a particular trader. Without storage or cash to wait, they may sell immediately after harvest when prices are low.',
'These weaknesses justify intervention: improving infrastructure and fair dealing increases the share of the final price received by producers and reduces waste. Credit and marketing are linked because urgent repayment can force a distress sale.')
L(5,'5.2 Measures to Improve Agricultural Marketing','Four groups of government measures',
'**Regulated markets:** market committees and rules improve weighing, transparency and trading practices, reducing manipulation. **Physical infrastructure:** roads, rail links, warehouses, cold storage and processing facilities reduce transport costs, spoilage and forced sale.',
'**Cooperative marketing:** farmers combine produce and bargain collectively, sharing services and reducing intermediary dependence. Gujarat’s milk cooperatives illustrate its potential; weak finance, coordination and management can limit success.',
'**Support-price and distribution measures:** MSP provides a declared support benchmark for specified crops; procurement by agencies such as FCI contributes to buffer stocks; and the PDS distributes essential goods to eligible households through fair-price shops. These are distinct functions—price support, stock holding and consumer distribution—not three names for the same operation.')
L(5,'5.2 Emerging Marketing Channels','Direct markets and contracts with firms',
'**Direct farmer–consumer markets** reduce intermediary stages and can improve the producer’s share while offering consumers fresher produce. Examples include Apni Mandi in Punjab and Haryana, Rythu Bazaars in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, and Uzhavar Sandies in Tamil Nadu.',
'**Contract arrangements with firms** can specify price, quality and purchase conditions before production. Firms may provide inputs, technical knowledge or assured markets, reducing some uncertainty for farmers.',
'Benefits depend on fair terms and enforcement. A small farmer may have weaker bargaining power than a large buyer, and rejection or quality disputes can create new risks. Explain both the opportunity and the conditions needed for it to work.')
L(5,'5.3 Diversification of Agricultural Activities','Two dimensions of diversification',
'**Crop diversification** means growing a wider variety of crops rather than relying on one crop or the traditional pattern. It can spread weather and price risks and open opportunities in higher-value crops. Subsistence farmers may hesitate because familiar food crops provide household security.',
'**Diversification of productive activity** means moving some labour and resources from cultivation into allied and non-farm activities. Animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services create additional sources of income.',
'Benefits include higher and more stable earnings, productive use of surplus or disguised-unemployed labour, balanced rural development and less pressure to migrate to cities. Diversification should create viable opportunities rather than merely move people into another low-income activity.')
L(5,'5.3 Animal Husbandry · Operation Flood','Livestock and the White Revolution',
'**Animal husbandry** includes rearing livestock for milk, meat, eggs, wool and other products. Mixed crop–livestock farming provides income at different times and can support households in dry regions. Women and landless households often participate, making livestock important for inclusive rural livelihoods.',
'Constraints include low animal productivity, inadequate knowledge, poor breeds, insufficient credit and limited veterinary care. Better breeding, feed, disease control, training and market access can raise returns.',
'**Operation Flood**, launched in **1970**, organised milk collection, grading, processing and distribution through cooperative networks. Pooling milk and linking rural producers with urban markets improved bargaining and income opportunities. The resulting expansion is associated with the **White Revolution**, with Gujarat’s cooperatives a prominent example.',kind='Concept and box')
L(5,'5.3 Fisheries and Horticulture','Two other sources of rural employment',
'**Fisheries:** inland fishing uses rivers, canals, ponds and other inland waters; marine fishing uses seas and oceans. Fishing and related processing and marketing support livelihoods, including women’s employment. Poverty, indebtedness, inadequate technology and finance constrain producers, while overfishing and pollution threaten sustainability.',
'**Horticulture** includes fruits, vegetables, flowers, plantation crops, nurseries and related activities. Diverse climatic conditions create opportunities for higher-value production and employment. The chapter’s **Golden Revolution** discussion links research and technology transfer with expansion in fruits, vegetables and honey.',
'Horticulture needs irrigation, reliable electricity, cold chains, transport, processing, research and market information because many products perish quickly. Switching acreage can also reduce production of staples such as pulses. Assess profitability alongside nutrition, risk and environmental needs.')
L(5,'5.3 Women, Information Technology and Model Villages','TANWA, rural information and SAGY',
'**Tamil Nadu Women in Agriculture (TANWA)** trained women in improved agricultural practices. Better skills raised productivity and encouraged savings, groups and enterprises such as mushroom production and small manufactures. The example links training to both earnings and empowerment.',
'**Information technology** can provide weather forecasts, soil advice, prices, employment information and access to government services. Rural information kiosks and the National Virtual Academy for Rural Prosperity illustrate connecting villages with useful knowledge. Information helps only when it is timely, understandable and supported by access to the relevant service.',
'**Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY)**, launched in October 2014, encourages MPs to develop model villages through local participation and coordinated improvements in health, nutrition, education and livelihoods. The chapter describes indicative village populations of 3,000–5,000 in plains and 1,000–3,000 in hilly areas, and selection outside the MP’s own or spouse’s village.',kind='Important boxes')
L(5,'5.4 Organic Farming','Meaning and five advantages of organic farming',
'**Organic farming** relies on ecological processes, organic manure, compost and biological methods to maintain soil health and manage pests, reducing reliance on synthetic chemical inputs. Its aim is sustainable production that protects the resource base for future generations.',
'**Reduced dependence on non-renewable inputs:** many synthetic inputs rely on petroleum-based production. **Environmental protection:** less chemical runoff can reduce soil and groundwater pollution. **Soil fertility:** organic matter helps maintain soil structure and biological activity.',
'**Food and health appeal:** consumers may value reduced chemical residues and product quality, though organic status alone does not guarantee every nutritional claim. **Suitability for small producers:** locally available inputs and labour-based methods can reduce dependence on purchased chemicals. These benefits depend on appropriate practices, rather than merely stopping fertiliser use.')
L(5,'5.4 Organic Farming · Prospects and Challenges','Exports, transition costs and the cotton example',
'India’s abundant labour can be an advantage in labour-intensive organic production. Certified products may obtain premium prices and export opportunities. However, certification, consumer trust, market connections and practical knowledge are necessary to realise those gains.',
'**Challenges:** yields can fall during transition; lower output can raise cost per unit; products may have a shorter shelf life; certification and marketing can be difficult; and farmers need training and suitable inputs. A sudden universal switch without support can threaten farm incomes and food availability.',
'The Maharashtra organic-cotton case involving Kisan Mehta and Prakruti illustrates farmers experimenting with chemical-free production and international standards. The broader lesson is to combine soil protection with economic viability. The chapter also discusses **PM-PRANAM** as an initiative encouraging alternative fertilisers and balanced fertiliser use; organic farming is part of the wider sustainability agenda.',kind='Concept and box')
# CHAPTER 6 — Employment, all distinctions, trends and programmes.
L(6,'6.1 Workers and Employment','Who counts as a worker?',
'A **worker** contributes to the production of goods and services counted in economic activity. Workers include employees, self-employed people and unpaid helpers in family enterprises. Payment may be in cash or kind, and someone working for only part of the year can still be a worker.',
'A farmer, street vendor, teacher and unpaid family-business helper can all be workers. Being a consumer does not itself make a person a worker. Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are generally excluded from measured economic production, although they are valuable work in everyday life.',
'Employment is studied to understand the quantity and quality of human resources, sectoral contributions, job security, exploitation, child labour and training needs. People work for income and survival, but also for self-worth, participation and contribution to society.')
L(6,'6.1 Self-employed and Hired Workers','Three employment statuses',
'**Self-employed workers** operate their own enterprises or farms and earn returns from that activity rather than receiving a wage from an employer. **Regular salaried employees** work under a continuing employment arrangement, usually with more predictable pay and conditions.',
'**Casual wage labourers** are hired for short periods or particular tasks, often on daily wages, without assured continuing work. Construction labour and seasonal agricultural labour are common examples. Regular status usually offers better security, but the actual benefits depend on the contract and enforcement.',
'Employment status is different from industry. A person in manufacturing could be self-employed, regularly employed or casually hired; do not treat “factory worker” as automatically a secure salaried job.')
L(6,'6.1 Labour Supply, Labour Force and Workforce','People available, people employed and hours supplied',
'**Labour force** consists of employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work and seeking it under the relevant measurement definition. **Workforce** consists only of the employed. People neither employed nor seeking or available for work are outside the labour force.',
'**Labour supply** concerns the amount of work offered, measured in worker-hours or worker-days. The same number of people can supply more labour by working longer hours. Thus a change in hours need not mean a change in the number of people in the labour force.',
'Keep denominators separate: unemployment is measured relative to the labour force, while worker–population ratio relates workers to the population. A student not seeking work is not automatically unemployed.',formula='Labour force = employed + unemployed\nUnemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force × 100\nWorker–population ratio = workforce ÷ population × 100')
L(6,'6.2 Participation in Employment · WPR','Worker–population ratio and rural–urban differences',
'**Worker–population ratio (WPR)** is the percentage of the population engaged in work. It indicates participation in measured employment, not the quality, productivity or pay of that work. A higher WPR does not by itself establish greater prosperity.',
'The textbook’s **2022–23** figures are: rural men 54%, urban men 55.6%, all men 54.4%; rural women 30%, urban women 18.7%, all women 27%; and total rural 42.3%, urban 37.7%, all persons 41.1%. These are a dated comparison, not current live statistics.',
'Rural participation may be higher because poverty pushes people into work earlier and fewer people can remain in education or wait for suitable jobs. Urban opportunities for study and job search can reduce measured participation. Interpret rates together with age, education, income and the character of employment.')
L(6,'6.2 Participation · Gender and Employment Status','Why participation differs between groups',
'Men’s measured participation is higher than women’s because of unequal educational access, household responsibilities, social restrictions, limited suitable opportunities and under-recognition of women’s productive work. Rural women may participate in family farms or enterprises; such productive assistance should be counted even without a separate wage.',
'Rural employment is dominated by self-employment because many households cultivate land or run small activities and have limited salaried opportunities. Urban areas offer more regular employment in firms, offices and services. Casual labour remains important where people lack assets, skills or secure contracts.',
'Women can be concentrated in home-based, traditional or low-paid activities because mobility and opportunities are restricted. Do not confuse unpaid family-enterprise work, which is economic activity, with own-household domestic services, which are generally outside the measured production boundary.')
L(6,'6.3 Employment in Different Sectors','Industry groups and sectoral distribution',
'**Primary sector:** agriculture and allied activities, mining and quarrying. **Secondary sector:** manufacturing, electricity/gas/water supply and construction. **Tertiary sector:** trade, transport, storage and other services. These groups classify what workers produce, rather than their employment status.',
'The chapter’s **2022–23 national distribution** is about **46.1% primary, 24.9% secondary and 29% tertiary**. Rural employment depends more heavily on agriculture, while urban employment is concentrated in industry and services. Women are particularly concentrated in agricultural and family-based work.',
'Agriculture’s large employment share alongside a much smaller output share suggests low average output per worker and pressure on land. Structural development requires productive opportunities outside farming, rather than displacement without alternative livelihoods.')
L(6,'6.3 Changing Employment Structure','A slow shift and new ways of working',
'The textbook compares **1972–73** employment shares of about **74.3% primary, 10.9% secondary and 14.8% tertiary** with the later distribution. The primary share has fallen and the other shares have risen, but agriculture remains the largest source of employment.',
'This is a structural shift, though slower than the transformation of output. A growing services share in GDP need not absorb all workers leaving farming, especially when expanding services require skills that many workers lack.',
'Home-based work is not entirely new: weaving and other household production have long existed. Digital technology expanded remote work, particularly during the COVID-19 period. Its opportunities depend on connectivity, skills and the nature of the job.',kind='Concept and box')
L(6,'6.4 Growth and Employment','Jobless growth and the output–employment gap',
'**Jobless growth** means output grows without a corresponding expansion in employment. It does not necessarily mean literally zero jobs are created; the concern is that employment growth is inadequate relative to output growth and the number seeking work.',
'Capital-intensive technology, automation and efficiency improvements can increase production using relatively fewer workers. Growth concentrated in high-skill activities may not provide suitable opportunities for the wider labour force. Some existing jobs can also disappear during restructuring.',
'To evaluate growth, ask how many jobs it creates, who can access them, and whether they provide productive, secure livelihoods. Labour-intensive activities, skills, infrastructure and support for smaller enterprises help connect output growth with employment.')
L(6,'6.4 Casualisation of Workforce','What casualisation means—and what the trend actually shows',
'**Casualisation** is a shift towards casual wage work relative to self-employment or regular salaried work. Workers may face uncertain hours, weak bargaining power, easy dismissal and limited benefits. Rural surplus labour migrating without adequate skills can enter such work; educated workers may also accept insecure contracts when alternatives are scarce.',
'The textbook’s series is not a continuous rise: the casual share increased from **23.2% in 1972–73** to **31.8% in 1993–94**, but was around **30% in 2011–12** and **23.3% in 2022–23**. State the relevant period rather than claiming that casualisation increased in every year.',
'The concept concerns the quality and composition of work. A rise in total employment can coexist with insecurity if new jobs are predominantly casual or poorly protected.')
L(6,'6.5 Formal and Informal Sectors','Organised versus unorganised employment',
'The textbook classifies **all public-sector establishments and private establishments employing ten or more workers** as the formal or organised sector. Smaller private enterprises and many own-account activities belong to the informal or unorganised sector.',
'Formal employment is more likely to have written conditions, regular pay, social-security benefits and enforceable protection. Informal workers often face uncertain earnings, weak implementation of safeguards, limited collective bargaining and greater exposure to market fluctuations. This does not mean informal workers have no legal rights.',
'The chapter’s **2011–12** comparison puts roughly **30 million workers, or 6%, in the formal sector**, and **443 million, or 94%, in the informal sector**. These are historical textbook figures. A worker’s actual job protection should still be examined; establishment size alone does not guarantee every benefit.')
L(6,'6.5 Informalisation · Cases and Labour Codes','Why protection matters',
'**Informalisation** describes the predominance or spread of employment outside effectively protected formal arrangements. Workers may be employed yet remain vulnerable to poverty, accidents, illness and loss of work. Social security, skills and enforceable conditions therefore matter alongside the number of jobs.',
'The **Ahmedabad textile-mill case** describes large-scale closures and displacement from previously organised employment. Loss of wages and benefits pushed households towards insecure work and financial distress. The lesson is that industrial restructuring needs support for workers’ transition.',
'The textbook’s **labour-code box** highlights social protection for gig and platform workers, appointment letters and clearly stated terms, health checks for workers over 40, and safeguards for women working at night. These illustrate efforts to extend protection and improve working conditions rather than merely count employment.',kind='Important box')
L(6,'6.6 Unemployment · Meaning and Measurement','Who is unemployed and where data come from',
'**Unemployment** occurs when people willing and able to work at the prevailing terms cannot obtain work. Someone choosing not to seek work is different from someone seeking a job unsuccessfully. An employed person in unsuitable or insufficient work may face underemployment rather than open unemployment.',
'The chapter identifies the **Census**, employment surveys of the national statistical system, including the **Periodic Labour Force Survey**, and **employment-exchange registrations** as sources of information. Registered applicants are only a subset of all unemployed people; survey definitions and reference periods also matter.',
'To interpret a figure, ask whether it measures people, days or hours and what willingness, availability and job-search criteria were used. Do not calculate the unemployment rate by dividing unemployed people by the entire population.')
L(6,'6.6 Disguised and Seasonal Unemployment','Two important rural forms',
'**Disguised unemployment:** more people are engaged in an activity than are required, so some can be withdrawn without reducing total output. Their marginal contribution is effectively zero. A joint-family farm may divide a fixed amount of work among many members when alternative jobs are unavailable.',
'**Seasonal unemployment:** work is available during particular seasons but not throughout the year. A single-crop area may provide work during sowing and harvesting while leaving workers idle in the off-season. Similar patterns can occur in seasonal processing or construction activities.',
'Disguised unemployment concerns excess workers at a given time; seasonal unemployment concerns lack of work during part of the year. Irrigation, multiple cropping, allied activities and non-farm employment can help. Disguised unemployment can also occur outside agriculture when enterprises are overstaffed.')
L(6,'6.6 Causes of Unemployment','Eight causes and their mechanisms',
'**Slow economic growth:** output and investment may not expand enough to absorb jobseekers. **Rapid population growth:** the labour force can grow faster than employment opportunities. **Seasonal agriculture:** limited irrigation and single cropping leave long off-seasons. **Joint-family system:** workers remain attached to a farm despite insufficient productive work.',
'**Decline of cottage and small industries:** loss of traditional livelihoods is not always offset by new opportunities. **Low investment:** inadequate capital formation limits enterprises and job creation. **Capital-intensive technology:** output can rise with relatively little additional labour.',
'**Low mobility of labour:** language, family ties, social conditions, information and moving costs restrict movement to available jobs. These causes can reinforce each other, so remedies must address both labour demand and people’s ability to reach and perform the jobs.')
L(6,'6.7 Government and Employment Generation','Direct and indirect employment',
'**Direct employment:** government departments and public enterprises hire workers to provide administration, education, health, transport, infrastructure and other services. These workers are employed by the public institution itself.',
'**Indirect employment:** public production, procurement and infrastructure create opportunities elsewhere. A public steel plant supplies inputs to downstream private industries, while roads make new businesses viable. Suppliers, contractors and linked enterprises can employ additional workers.',
'Government also promotes self-employment, credit, skills and wage-work programmes. Distinguish an immediate public job from a policy that enables another employer or an individual enterprise to create work.')
L(6,'6.7 Employment Programmes · Historical Framework','Self-employment, rural enterprises and roads',
'**PMRY (1993):** assisted educated unemployed youth in establishing enterprises through credit and support. **SJSRY (1997):** addressed urban poverty through wage employment and self-employment arrangements. **REGP (1995):** promoted rural industries and artisan employment.',
'**PMEGP (2008):** a credit-linked subsidy programme supporting micro-enterprises in manufacturing and services. **SGSY (1999):** promoted rural self-employment through groups, credit and assistance; its approach was subsequently restructured under **NRLM/Aajeevika**, with an emphasis on sustainable livelihoods.',
'**PMGSY (December 2000):** all-weather rural road connectivity improves access to markets, services and employment. These programme names belong to different periods; learn each programme’s mechanism and distinguish a historical scheme from its successor. PMEGP should not be described as discontinued simply because a textbook passage groups it with older schemes.')
L(6,'6.7 Rural Employment Guarantee','MGNREGA and the textbook’s updated guarantee box',
'**MGNREGA, 2005**, historically guaranteed **100 days of wage employment per rural household per financial year** to adult members volunteering for unskilled manual work. It is a household guarantee, not 100 days separately for every adult. Its framework included employment within the prescribed period, unemployment allowance where applicable, and a minimum one-third participation provision for women.',
'The purpose is livelihood security combined with useful rural assets and local work opportunities. It helps households during agricultural slack periods and can strengthen workers’ bargaining power.',
'The chapter’s newer box discusses the **Viksit Bharat—Guarantee for Rozgar and Ajeevika Mission (Gramin) Act, 2025**, which replaces the earlier framework and raises the guarantee to **125 days per rural household**. Keep the date and framework explicit: answer 100 days when asked about MGNREGA’s original provision, and 125 days when asked about the updated Act.',kind='Concept and updated box')
L(6,'6.7 Recent Initiatives · Credit, Skills and Enterprise','MUDRA, skills, Make in India and Start-up India',
'**MUDRA**, launched in 2015, supports credit for eligible small and micro enterprises, enabling self-employment and expansion. **PMKVY** supports industry-relevant skills through the skills-development framework and training partners. Credit and training work best when supported by markets and viable business opportunities.',
'**Make in India**, launched on 25 September 2014, seeks to encourage production, investment and manufacturing activity with employment opportunities. **Start-up India** encourages entrepreneurship and enterprise creation, which can generate jobs for founders and others.',
'**DAY-NULM** promotes urban poor households’ livelihoods through skills, wage employment and self-employment support. These programmes use different channels—finance, skills, investment and enterprise—so an answer should connect each name to its employment mechanism rather than merely list schemes.')
L(6,'6.7 Recent Initiatives · Employment Incentives','COVID-era support and employer incentives',
'**Atmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY, 2020)** used employment-related incentives to encourage new hiring or re-employment during recovery from COVID disruption. **Pradhan Mantri Rojgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY, 2016)** supported eligible employer contributions to encourage formal job creation; the chapter’s account refers to a defined registration and benefit period.',
'**Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan**, launched on 20 June 2020, was a **125-day campaign** supporting livelihoods and rural works for returning migrants and affected rural households. Its 125-day campaign duration is different from a household’s annual legal employment guarantee under the later Act.',
'The wider challenge is to create productive work while improving security, matching skills with demand and extending opportunities to women and disadvantaged groups. Employment policy succeeds when people can earn a sustainable livelihood, not simply when a scheme has been announced.',kind='Important box')
